> The name "拔丝" literally means "pulled silk"—and that's exactly what happens when you lift a piece from the plate. The sugar coating stretches into fine threads that make this dessert as fun to eat as it is delicious. Work quickly when assembling, as the coating sets fast.

== Prepare the Sweet Potato ==

Peel @sweet potatoes{500%g} (about 2 medium) and cut into 1 cm thick rounds, then into 1 cm thick sticks. Pat thoroughly dry—any moisture will cause the frying oil to splatter and the coating to be soggy.

== Fry the Sweet Potato ==

Heat @oil{500%ml} in a #wok{} or deep pot to 150°C (300°F).

Carefully add the sweet potato sticks in batches—don't crowd them or they'll steam instead of fry. Fry until they're just cooked through and pale, about ~{3-4%minutes}. They won't be golden at this stage.

Remove with a slotted spoon and drain on paper towels. Let rest for ~{5%minutes} to cool slightly.

== Make the Sugar Glaze ==

In a clean #wok{} (or the same wok with oil drained), combine:
- @white sugar{150%g}
- @water{60%ml}

Heat over medium heat, stirring only until the sugar dissolves. Once boiling, stop stirring—you want to cook the sugar without agitation to prevent crystallization.

Watch carefully as the syrup goes from clear to light amber to amber. Swirl the wok gently (don't stir) as it approaches 160°C (320°F). When it turns a deep amber color and smells of caramel, remove from heat immediately.

The color should be amber with a slight reddish tint—past this point, the sugar will burn quickly.

== Coat and Serve ==

Working quickly (the sugar sets in seconds), add the fried sweet potato sticks to the caramel wok. Toss gently to coat each piece evenly.

For added visual appeal and texture, sprinkle @sesame seeds{1%tbsp} over the coated sticks and toss again.

Transfer to a oiled plate or serving platter immediately—the coating will stick to anything it touches as it cools.

Serve immediately while the threads still stretch. Provide a bowl of cold water alongside for dipping fingers if needed (the sugar is dangerously hot when just made).